  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Intermittent light rain mixed with snow, mainly\nnorth of Bangor, through today. Snow accumulations will be \nzero except across the higher terrain.\n\nA weak low to the north will move east of the region bringing\npotential for light rain/snow showers this afternoon and early\nevening. CAPE values between 20-50 j/kg and modest mid-level \nlapse rates may help increase coverage during the late morning \nand afternoon hours. Therefore, little to no accumulation is \nexpected. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Confidence for impacts regarding the Costa \nstorm is decreasing, however, uncertainty still exists along the\nimmediate Downeast Coast.\n\nThursday afternoon an area of low pressure will develop off the\nmid-Atlantic coast and travel eastward. The overall trend for\nthe system the past 24 hours has been suppressed further as\nnortherly flow advects relatively dry air in the region late\nThursday into Friday. The combination of the southern New\nEngland track and dry air entrainment will keep the\nprecipitation shield primarily south of Bangor along the\nimmediate Downeast Coast. If any location will get any snow wil\nwill be late Thursday night into Friday morning along the route\n1 corridor in Hancock and Washington Counties, but even here it\nwill be a light coating at the most. The upper level feature\nwith the system will travel overhead through the weekend,\nallowing for diurnal rain/snow showers to develop keeping the\narea under mostly cloudy skies. Accumulations look to be very\nlight through this period, with no drought relief in sight.",
